  <title>2.10.2: Using Inequalities to Solve a Problem</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00543</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.10.2: Using Inequalities to Solve a Problem</md:title>
    <md:uuid>89998b50-7119-4a92-9c28-086a05a2dfab</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>Use the following information to answer questions 1 - 2.</para>
      <para>
        <quote>Han is about to mow some lawns in his neighborhood. His lawn mower has a 5-gallon fuel tank, but Han is not sure how much gasoline is in the tank.</quote>
        <quote>He knows, however, that the lawn mower uses 0.4 gallon of gasoline per hour of mowing.</quote>
        <quote>
          <figure>
            <media alt="A lawnmower on a lawn.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_12_03_01.jpg"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
          <emphasis/>
        </quote>
     </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>What are all the possible values for \(x\), the number of hours Han can mow without refilling the lawn mower?</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x\;\leq\;12.5,\;\) and \(x\;\geq\;0\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write one or more inequalities to represent your response. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x\leq\frac5{0.4}\) or \(x\leq12.5\) and \(x\geq0\). If the tank is completely full, Han can mow for at most 12.5 hours without refilling. If it is all the way empty, he can mow for 0 hours. It is not possible for Han to mow for a negative number of hours.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <title>Video: Using Inequalities to Solve a Problem</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about how to solve a problem using inequalities.</para>
